  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Existing Components**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the existing codebase, specifically `src/App.tsx`. I\'m mapping out the login and sign-up logic and how it routes to the dashboards. My primary goal is to identify any functional gaps or missing details within these components.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Missing Functionality**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on missing features based on user feedback. The map visibility issue is my immediate concern, and I\'m diving into `src/components/CitizenDashboard.tsx` to troubleshoot. I\'m also cross-referencing this with the `server.ts` file to see how those components interact on the backend. My current focus is to identify the root cause, and then to outline and plan for solutions. This should address the missing map, user interface, and overall user flow.\n\n\n**Investigating Map Visibility**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the Leaflet map implementation. I\'ve pinpointed the CSS import and the defined height/width within the `CitizenDashboard.tsx` component. My focus is on the parent containers, specifically those using `flex` and height declarations. I\'m cross-referencing this with the `AuthorityDashboard` component (in `Dashboard.tsx`) to explore other rendering scenarios. I\'m also considering potential conflicts with flex containers and Leaflet\'s loading behavior, and if there may be styling issues. I\'m considering all these to explain why the map might not be rendering.\n\n\n**Verifying Tile Loading**\n\nI\'m now investigating Leaflet\'s tile layer loading, specifically the URL for the base map. I\'ve confirmed the Leaflet styles are imported, and I am now considering the initial `location` state, which is initialized to `null`. I\'ve also identified the asynchronous nature of the geolocation call and its potential impact on map rendering.\n\n\n**Confirming Geolocation Delay**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the issue! If `navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition` hangs, `location` remains null, displaying \"Locating you...\" indefinitely. The map remains invisible because it\'s waiting for geolocation data. This explains the \"still map not visible on page\" problem. To fix this, I\'ll initialize the location with a default city coordinate and update it asynchronously if geolocation is successful. This should ensure immediate map visibility, which is exactly the user experience we want.\n\n\n**Implementing Default Location**\n\nI\'ve made a key discovery: the map was invisible because `navigator.geolocation` can block. I\'m now modifying `CitizenDashboard.tsx` so the map defaults to a known coordinate (`[12.687, 78.615]`), resolving the visibility issue immediately. I\'m also ensuring the map remains visible while geolocating and only pans once data is received. I\'m also taking a look at the login functionality!\n\nI\'ve also started reviewing the login and registration flows in `src/App.tsx`. I see how user state and authentication views are managed, focusing on user registration through `loginEmail`, `loginRole`, `name`, `role`, and `language` which is initialized. I\'m investigating how those components function together in `src/App.tsx`.\n\n\n**Addressing Language Mismatch**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical language issue: the login flow forces the language to English, ignoring the user\'s initial selection during signup. This directly contradicts the multi-language feature request. The \"functional of add\" refers to adding a new civic issue, and now I\'m searching for a manual issue reporting option or form within the app. I\'m investigating how a citizen can manually add civic issues, which is missing from the voice reporter! I am starting with the `VoiceReporter` component.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view the `VoiceReporter.tsx` component to inspect the manual report submission form and see how we can make it fully robust, and support manually adding issues with proper parameters."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 655100177
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
