  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing New Endpoint**\n\nI\'ve been working on a new `/api/issues` POST route. The goal is to allow manual creation of standard issues, separate from the civic report processing. I am designing the endpoint with `category`, `executive_summary`, and `severity` parameters. I\'ll need to define how this endpoint interacts with the issue database.\n\n\n**Implementing Issue Creation**\n\nI\'ve made significant progress implementing the `/api/issues` POST route. The endpoint now handles incoming data to create new issue entries. It addresses missing latitude/longitude, assigns a random issue ID, calculates the priority score, and defaults some fields. The code is complete and inserts new issues into the issue database. I\'m now testing the endpoint.\n\n\n**Refining UI Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating a manual issue creation flow into the `VoiceReporter.tsx` component. The current multi-step AI flow in `VoiceReporter.tsx` is being supplemented. I\'m considering three tab options for the UI: an AI Assistant, an AI Interactive option, and a \"Quick Post (Manual)\" option. This design aims to provide a faster, direct way to manually add issues, bypassing any transcription or AI analysis delays for the user.\n\n\n**Enhancing Issue Feed**\n\nI\'m now thinking about how to make the issue feed feel more like Reddit. Users need to be able to sort issues, with options like \"Top upvoted,\" \"Highest Priority,\" and \"Most Recent.\" Right now, it\'s just sorting by priority, but adding tabs to toggle between \"Trending (Priority),\" \"Popular (Most Upvotes),\" and \"Recent\" would be perfect. I\'m also thinking about adding filters by category, like \"Roads,\" \"Water Supply,\" etc., just like Reddit. I\'m taking a look at `CitizenDashboard.tsx` to understand how the feed works.\n\n\n"
